---
import LessonLayout from "../layouts/LessonLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import QuestionAnswer from "../components/QuestionAnswer.astro";
---

<LessonLayout
  title="Landscape perspective"
  description="Use camera position, distance and height to organise foreground, middle-ground and background in a landscape photograph."
>
  <h2>Build the scene in layers</h2>
  <p>
    A landscape often contains more than one subject. You may have rocks close
    to the camera, trees farther away and mountains on the horizon. Perspective
    controls how those layers relate in the photograph.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start by deciding which layer matters most. A large foreground can lead the
    viewer into the scene. A stronger background can give a mountain or storm
    more weight. Your camera position sets that balance before your lens frames
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>Give the foreground a purpose</h2>
  <p>
    Rocks, flowers, roots and shorelines can add depth, but they shouldn't be in
    the frame only because the space feels empty. Look for a shape, texture or
    line that helps guide attention towards the main subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move closer when you want the foreground to feel stronger. The nearby object
    will grow faster in the frame than a distant ridge. Take a small step, check
    the change and stop before the foreground hides useful parts of the scene.
  </p>
  <p>
    Step back when a rock or branch takes too much attention. This reduces its
    size compared with the background. You can then use a longer focal length to
    tighten the framing without returning to the same perspective.
  </p>

  <h2>Use the middle-ground to connect the scene</h2>
  <p>
    The middle-ground helps the eye travel between near and far subjects. A
    path, stream, stand of trees or patch of light can provide that connection.
    If the photograph feels split into two unrelated parts, check what happens
    between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Raising the camera may reveal more of a path or river behind a foreground
    object. Lowering it may hide that detail and make the foreground more
    dominant. Neither choice is always better. Choose the view that makes the
    layers easier to understand.
  </p>

  <h2>Look for overlap before changing lenses</h2>
  <p>
    Overlap tells the viewer which object sits in front of another, but too much
    overlap can make a scene difficult to read. A tree touching a mountain peak
    may look accidental. Two rocks merging into one dark shape may lose their
    form.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move sideways and watch the nearby objects shift against the background.
    Close subjects move across your view more than distant subjects. A step to
    the left or right can separate a branch from a summit, open space around a
    tree or place a winding path between two rocks.
  </p>
  <Callout title="Keep the distinction clear">
    <p>
      Choose your camera position for perspective. Then choose your focal length
      for framing. Zooming from one spot can't reveal a feature hidden behind a
      nearby object.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Change height with care</h2>
  <p>
    A low viewpoint can make grasses, stones or flowers feel larger. It can also
    hide the middle-ground behind them. Before setting up a tripod, compare the
    scene from knee height, chest height and eye level.
  </p>
  <p>
    A higher viewpoint often reveals more ground and creates clearer separation
    between layers. It may also reduce the impact of a foreground subject. Keep
    the camera level while comparing heights so camera tilt doesn't confuse the
    result.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose distance before focal length</h2>
  <p>
    Camera distance changes the apparent size relationship between near and far
    objects. Focal length changes how much of the scene fits in the frame. These
    controls work together, but they don't do the same job.
  </p>
  <p>
    Walk until the foreground, middle-ground and background have the balance you
    want. Then select a focal length that includes the right area. If the frame
    is too wide, use a longer lens. If important edges are missing, use a
    shorter lens or reconsider your position.
  </p>

  <h2>Check the frame from edge to edge</h2>
  <p>
    Strong depth near the centre won't fix a distracting edge. Look for cut-off
    branches, bright gaps, partial rocks and objects that just touch the border.
    A small change in position can clean up several of these problems at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check the horizon too. Make sure it doesn't run through the top of a nearby
    subject or divide the photograph in an awkward place. Adjust camera height
    before tilting, then decide whether the extra ground or sky supports the
    scene.
  </p>

  <h2>Use a simple field routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Decide which subject should carry the photograph.</li>
    <li>Compare a closer and a farther camera position.</li>
    <li>Move sideways to improve overlap and separation.</li>
    <li>Compare two or three camera heights.</li>
    <li>Choose focal length after the layers work together.</li>
    <li>Check the edges, horizon and focus before taking the final image.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Make one change at a time. If you move, zoom and tilt together, it's harder
    to see which choice improved the photograph. A few careful comparisons are
    more useful than changing every setting quickly.
  </p>

  <h2>Practise in the simulator</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the default view and save it as comparison A. Move closer to make
    the rock stronger, then move sideways to change the overlap between the tree
    and mountain. Raise and lower the camera to see how the ground layers shift.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once the relationships look clear, adjust focal length to refine the frame.
    Save comparison B and look at the size of each layer, not only the overall
    crop. The simulator simplifies a real landscape, but the decision process is
    the same outdoors.
  </p>

  <QuestionAnswer question="Does a lower camera always improve depth?">
    <p>
      No. A lower viewpoint can emphasise nearby objects, but it can also hide
      useful middle-ground detail or merge subjects together. Compare it with a
      higher position and keep the one that shows the layers more clearly.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <QuestionAnswer question="Do I need something in every layer?">
    <p>
      No. A simple landscape can work with only a strong subject and clean
      background. Use foreground and middle-ground elements when they add useful
      structure, direction or scale. Leave them out when they compete with the
      main subject.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <QuestionAnswer question="How do I keep every layer sharp?">
    <p>
      Perspective and focus are separate decisions. Camera position arranges the
      layers, while aperture and focus distance affect sharpness. After choosing
      your composition, check focus from the nearest important detail to the
      farthest one and adjust if needed.
    </p>
  </QuestionAnswer>
  <p>
    <a href="/perspective-simulator/">Try the perspective simulator</a>
    {" or "}
    <a href="/perspective-challenges/">
      practise with the perspective challenges
    </a>
    .
  </p>
</LessonLayout>
